  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 180d0828f9cf423459360235cd6922f2, type: 3}
  m_Name: Brain
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::DeepVisionTutorialSO
  steps:
  - The canine brain is the central control organ of the nervous system, protected
    within the cranial cavity of the skull. It coordinates sensory processing, voluntary
    movement, autonomic regulation, learning, memory, and behavioral responses.
  - The cerebrum is the largest region of the canine brain and is divided into left
    and right hemispheres. It processes conscious thought, environmental awareness,
    emotional behavior, and interpretation of visual, auditory, and olfactory information.
  - The cerebellum is positioned caudally beneath the cerebrum and is responsible
    for balance, posture, and precision of muscular coordination. It continuously
    refines motor activity to maintain smooth and controlled movement.
  - The brainstem includes the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata, connecting
    the brain to the spinal cord. It regulates vital involuntary functions including
    respiration, heart rate, swallowing, and sleep-wake cycles.
  - "The canine olfactory bulb is proportionally larger than in humans, reflecting
    the dog\u2019s exceptional sense of smell. Olfactory receptor pathways enable
    advanced scent detection used in tracking, hunting, and environmental recognition."
